 \begin{abstract}
-Assuming six explicit finite computational assertions (C1--C6), we classify the interior stationary sets and the full-gradient boundary-trace zeros of this angle energy across all spheroid shapes. A numerical search finds an equatorial saddle circle in a prolate spheroid; how can one exclude a second circle or an off-equatorial critical point? For the squared radial--normal angle averaged against cone volume we reduce the full stationary-set diagram to six explicit computational assertions. In particular, rotation turns an entire critical orbit into a scalar root; a fourth derivative in a tangential direction determines its radial crossing. An exploratory transverse-sign cover left 21,254 of 31,340 leaves undecided. Two representations of a tangential fourth derivative give a negative upper bound on all 731 equatorial boxes for aspect ratios from two to five. A second implementation encloses the same boxes with 1,043 terminal pieces. Strict radial monotonicity then reduces the existence of a saddle circle to two endpoint signs. Analytically, the sphere has a unique interior stationary minimum, and for $0<\lambda\le 1/32$ the sole interior stationary point is the index-one saddle at the centre, with no full-gradient boundary zeros. Over the full volume-preserving family, analytic endpoint estimates and the six named inputs yield four transitions involving the centre, axial saddle pairs, an equatorial saddle circle and boundary gradient traces. The oblate window $\lambda\in[2/5,33/50]$ is separately verified by the V24 ruling, and the spherical case $\lambda=1$ is proved analytically without computational input.
+Assuming six explicit finite computational assertions (C1--C6), we classify the interior stationary sets and the full-gradient boundary-trace zeros of this angle energy across all spheroid shapes. A numerical search finds an equatorial saddle circle in a prolate spheroid; how can one exclude a second circle or an off-equatorial critical point? For the squared radial--normal angle averaged against cone volume we reduce the full stationary-set diagram to six explicit computational assertions. In particular, rotation turns an entire critical orbit into a scalar root; a fourth derivative in a tangential direction determines its radial crossing. An exploratory transverse-sign cover left 21,254 of 31,340 leaves undecided. Two representations of a tangential fourth derivative give a negative upper bound on all 731 equatorial boxes for aspect ratios from two to five. A second implementation encloses the same boxes with 1,043 terminal pieces. Strict radial monotonicity then reduces the existence of a saddle circle to two endpoint signs. Analytically, the sphere has a unique interior stationary minimum, and for $0<\lambda\le 1/32$ the sole interior stationary point is the index-one saddle at the centre, with no full-gradient boundary zeros. Over the full volume-preserving family, analytic endpoint estimates and the six named inputs yield four transitions involving the centre, axial saddle pairs, an equatorial saddle circle and boundary gradient traces. The oblate window $\lambda\in[2/5,33/50]$ is separately verified in the author's recorded audit (record V24; see Section~\ref{sec:availability}), and the spherical case $\lambda=1$ is proved analytically without computational input.
 \end{abstract}
 \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} spheroid; stationary set; cone volume measure; radial Gauss map; Morse--Bott critical manifold; validated numerics.
 
